    <header>
        <h1>What is Problematic Social Media Use?</h1>
    </header>

    <main>

        <section class="problem-grid">

            <div class="problem-box problematic">
                 <img src="/static/Social_Media_Image.jpg" alt="Social Media"> 
            </div>

            <div class="problem-box image">
                <span>Problematic social media use</span>

                <p>Problematic Social Media Use is defined as excessive 
                social media use that significantly disrupts school achievement, 
                relationships and overall wellbeing. It involves unhealthy online 
                habits, such as constantly checking notifications, scrolling for 
                long periods and struggling to reduce screen time. This can lead 
                to poor sleep, reduced concentration, lower academic performance 
                and increased anxiety. It becomes problematic when social media 
                interferes with daily responsibilities and negatively affects a 
                person's mental health and everyday life. It can also cause people 
                to spend less time on hobbies, physical activities and face-to-face 
                interactions. Over time, these habits can make it harder to maintain 
                a healthy balance between social media and other important aspects of 
                life.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="problem-box image">
            <img src="/static/Algorithm-Image.jpg" alt="Social Media Algorithm">
            </div>

            <div class="problem-box algorithm">
                <span>Algorithm</span>
                
                <p>Social media algorithms are tools 
                that analyze user behavior in terms of liking, 
                commenting, sharing, and viewing time in order 
                to provide users with recommendations that align 
                with their preferences. The algorithms are intended 
                to ensure engagement of the users by displaying posts 
                and videos which they are likely to react to. Such approach 
                could contribute to problematic social media use as it makes 
                users check social media more often and become glued to the 
                platforms. These algorithms can create a cycle where users 
                continuously scroll through personalised content, making it 
                difficult to stop using social media. Over time, this may negatively 
                affect their sleep, concentration, academic performance and mental 
                wellbeing.</p> 
            </div>

            <div class="problem-box myths">
    <table class="myths-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Myth</th>
                <th>Fact</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Social media is always bad for teenagers.</td>
                <td>Social media can help teenagers connect with friends, learn new things and find support. However, excessive use can affect wellbeing.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Spending lots of time online always means someone has a problem.</td>
                <td>Social media use becomes concerning when it interferes with sleep, schoolwork, relationships or daily responsibilities.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Social media cannot affect your sleep.</td>
                <td>Using social media late at night can reduce sleep time and disrupt healthy sleep routines.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
            <div class="problem-box scrolling">
                <span>“Scrolling our lives away” explanation</span>

                <p>Scrolling Our Lives Away refers to spending excessive 
                amounts of time scrolling through social media instead of 
                focusing on important daily activities. People may lose track of 
                time while watching videos or viewing posts, which can affect their 
                sleep, schoolwork, relationships and physical activities. Over time, 
                this habit can reduce productivity and lead to an unhealthy balance between 
                online and real-life experiences.</p>

                <p>Social media platforms often use algorithms to keep users interested by 
                constantly recommending new videos, posts and trends. This can make people 
                feel the need to keep scrolling because they do not want to miss anything 
                interesting. To prevent this, users can set screen time limits, take regular 
                breaks and focus on offline activities such as sports, hobbies and spending 
                time with family and friends. Developing these habits can help people use social 
                media responsibly while maintaining a healthier balance in their daily lives.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="problem-box evidence">
                <span>Experts Opinions on "Scrolling our Lives away" statement</span>

                <p>1. Dr Anne Lembkes central opinion is that digital devices act like 
                "the modern equivalent of the hypoderemic needle," delivering a contiuos drip 
                of digital drugs. She explains that social media and other digital activities 
                stimulate the brain’s reward system, encouraging users to seek instant gratification. 
                Over time, this can lead to excessive screen time, making it harder to disconnect and 
                affecting sleep, schoolwork and relationships.</p> 

                <p>2. Tech ethicists Tristan Harris and Aza Raskin focus on how social media platforms 
                are deliberately designed to capture and maintain users' attention. They explain that 
                features such as infinite scrolling, autoplay and personalised recommendations encourage 
                people to spend more time online. These features can make it difficult for users to stop 
                scrolling, potentially contributing to problematic social media use and negatively affecting 
                their daily lives.</p> 
            </div>

            <div class="problem-box summary">
                <span>My Summary</span>

                <p>I personally think that we are a bit too addicted to social media </p> 
            </div>

        </section>

